## Selective Epitaxial Growth of Oriented Hierarchical MetalOrganic Framework Heterostructures
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Heterostructures with precisely defined compositions, morphologies and interfaces are important for many applications including electronics,1 catalysis,2 and energy.3 Till now, various heterostructures with diverse architectures and dimensionalities have been constructed using the zerodimensional (0D) nanoclusters/nanoparticles, one-dimensional (1D) nanorods/nanowires, and two-dimensional (2D) nanosheets as building blocks.4-10 For example, a library of complex 0D, 1D and 2D metal chalcogenide heterostructures have been prepared by controlled cation exchange reactions.4 In addition, branched heterostructures have been obtained by the secondary epitaxial growth of 1D nanorods or 2D nanosheets on the nanowire backbones.6, 7 Recently, complex 1D/2D heterostructures with specific architectures have also been prepared by controlled epitaxial growth of 1D nanorods on the basal planes and side facets of 2D nanoplates.10 However, such rational design and synthesis of complex hierarchical heterostructures mainly focus on metals, metal chalcogenides and metal oxides.11 The controlled synthesis of metal-organic framework (MOF)-based heterostructures are still scarce.
MOFs are one kind of porous materials constructed by connecting metal ions/clusters with bidentate/polydentate organic ligands.12-17 Owing to their unique features including large surface areas, high pore volumes, and tunable structures, lots of efforts have been devoted to prepare MOF heterostructures by integrating MOFs with other materials.18-23 Particularly, the MOF/MOF heterostructures, which are formed by growing one kind of MOFs onto another, have shown various promising applications including gas adsorption,24 gas separation,25 catalysis,26, 27 and sensing,28 owing to the synergistic effect among different MOFs. To date, many strategies have been developed to synthesize MOF/MOF heterostructures, including epitaxial growth method,29-36 surfactant-assisted method,27, 37 kinetic control method,26, 38, 39 surface functionalization and retrosynthetic method,40 and ligand exchange method.41 However, most of the obtained heterostructures are core-shell structures, in which the secondary MOF was uniformly coated on another MOF. As a typical example, core-shell MOF heterostructures were prepared by growing Ga-MIL-88B on Fe-MIL-88B hexagonal nanorods, owing to the well-matched lattices.42 However, sandwich-type nanorods were obtained by the selective growth of In-MIL-88B on the tip of Fe-MIL-88B nanorods, due to their smaller lattice mismatch on the tip than that on the side.42 Importantly, the selective growth of MOFs with specific orientations resulted in promising applications in optics,43, 44 selective adsorption,45 electrochemistry46 and water evaporation.47 Unfortunately, such kind of controlled synthesis of MOF/MOF heterostructures has been rarely reported. The major synthetic barrier originates from difficult control on the size and shape of each MOF component, as well as the selective nucleation and growth of the secondary MOFs. Particularly, it remains a big challenge to precisely control the growth of the secondary MOFs on the selective surface of another MOF to construct hierarchical heterostructures with specific architectures, dimensionalities and spatial orientations.
Here, we report the controlled synthesis of a series of oriented hierarchical MOF heterostructures via a seeded epitaxial growth method. Typically, epitaxial MOF heterostructures are mainly constructed from isostructural MOFs with identical ligand length and topological structure. Owing to their perfectly matched lattices, core-shell MOF heterostructures are formed.29-36 In order to prepare oriented hierarchical MOF heterostructures, MOF seeds formed with different ligands and topological structures were dedicatedly chosen to match the lattice of the secondary MOFs to control their nucleation and growth. As the result, the secondary MOFs can only epitaxially grow on the selective surface of the MOF seeds with smaller lattice mismatch, forming the hierarchical MOF heterostructures with specific orientations.
To demonstrate this concept, three kinds of MOF nanostructures with different size, shapes,
and dimensionalities i.e., the 0D PCN-608 nanoparticles, 1D NU-1000 nanorods, and 2D PCN-134 nanoplates, were synthesized (see the detailed Experimental Section in Supporting Information), which were subsequently used as seeds for the epitaxial growth of PCN-222 nanorods. Due to different lattice mismatches between PCN-222 and the MOF seeds, three kinds of hierarchical MOF heterostructures were obtained, i.e., 1D PCN-222 nanorods grown along the major axis of the ellipsoid-like 0D PCN-608 nanoparticles (denoted as 1D/0D PCN-222/PCN-608), 1D PCN-222 (also known as MOF-545) nanorods grown on two end facets of 1D NU-1000 nanorods (denoted as 1D/1D PCN-222/NU-1000), and 1D PCN-222 nanorods vertically grown on two basal planes of 2D PCN-134 nanoplates (denoted as 1D/2D PCN-222/PCN-134) (Figure S1). Furthermore, another oriented hierarchical MOF heterostructure was also synthesized, in which $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB $\mathrm{BTB=}$ benzene tribenzoate) nanosheets selectively grew on six edge facets of 2D PCN-134 nanoplates, resulting in the formation of 2D/2D $Z\mathbf{r}$ -BTB/PCN-134 heterostructure (right panel in Figure S1c). It is worth mentioning that the synthesized MOF nanostructures in this work possess the same crystal structures with their bulk MOF counterparts.48-53 Such highly controlled synthesis relies on the rational design and engineering of the structures, sizes, dimensionalities, morphologies and lattice parameters of both the MOF seeds and the secondary MOFs.
## 2. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The crystal structures of the MOFs used for the synthesis of hierarchical heterostructures are listed in Figure S2. PCN-608, NU-1000 and PCN-222 crystalize in the space group of P6/mmm by linking 8-connected $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}$ clusters with TPCB-OH $(3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)-4,4'-dihydroxybiphenyl), TBAPy (1,3,6,8-tetrakis(4-benzoicacid)pyrene) and TCPP (tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin) ligands.48-51 PCN-134 crystalizes in the space group of $P6/$ mmm by connecting 10- connected $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}$ clusters with TCPP and BTB ligands.52 Zr-BTB crystalizes in space group of $P\overline{{3}}$ by linking 6-connected $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}$ clusters with BTB ligands.53 Due to the difference in topological structures and lattice parameters (Table S1), the secondary MOFs can only selectively and epitaxially grow on those surfaces with matched lattice of the MOF seeds, leading to the formation of oriented hierarchical heterostructures.
To synthesize $1\mathrm{D}/0\mathrm{D}$ heterostructures, the ellipsoid-like PCN-608 nanoparticles were chosen as seeds due to their anisotropic structure, which is essential for the selective epitaxial growth of the secondary PCN-222 nanorods on their surface (Figures 1a and S1a). The scanning electron microscope (SEM) and transmission electron microscope (TEM) characterizations clearly show the ellipsoid-like morphology of PCN-608 with the length of $320\pm25~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and width of $230\pm18\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure
S3a-d). The $X\mathrm{-}$ ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of PCN-608 nanoparticles matches well with the simulated data of PCN608 MOF (Figure S3e, f), confirming its crystal structure. Owing to the usage of difference ligands, the lattice parameters of PCN-608 seeds and PCN-222 are quite different (Figure S4a, b). However, the lattice mismatch between their parameter a (or $b$ ) is very small $(2.5~\%)$ , compared with that between their parameter $c$ $(11.0\%)$ . Therefore, it is possible to selectively grow PCN-222 along the $c$ axis of PCN-608, due to the smaller lattice mismatch. As expected, PCN-222 crystals selectively grew along the major axis of the ellipsoid-like PCN-608 nanoparticles, but not on the entire surface, to form PCN-222/PCN-608 heterostructures (Figure 1b, c). The TEM image of an individual heterostructure clearly shows the selective growth of PCN-222 nanorods on the PCN-608 nanoparticle (Figure 1c). HRTEM image and fast Fourier transform (FFT) were employed to characterize the crystallinity and growth mechanism of the heterostructures. The HRTEM image of the interface shows the lattice fringes of the two MOFs, indicating the crystalline structure of the heterostructure (Figure 1d). Moreover, the measured lattice fringes, $3.3~\mathrm{nm}$ , can be ascribed to the (010) planes of PCN608 and PCN-222.48, 50 The continuity of the lattice fringes across the interface of PCN-608 and PCN-222 demonstrates that the epitaxial relationship between them is $(010)_{\mathrm{PCN}}.$ - $_{608}\Vert(010)_{\mathrm{PCN}-222}$ . The FFT patterns taken at the PCN-608 and the interface are quite similar with the same orientation, further confirming the epitaxial growth of PCN-222 on PCN608 (Figure 1e). Finally, the crystal structure of 1D/0D PCN222/PCN-608 heterostructure was proved by the XRD and small angle X-ray scattering (SAXS) measurements. Due to overlap of the diffraction peaks, only some small peaks are assignable to PCN-222 nanorods (Figure S5). Furthermore, SAXS was also used to confirm the crystal structure of PCN222/PCN-608 heterostructure (Figure 1f), clearly showing the diffraction peaks of PCN-222 (marked with black stars) and PCN-608 (marked with red stars). Based on the aforementioned results, the structure model can be drawn, in which the PCN-222 crystals grow along the $c$ axis of PCN-608 with their (010) planes parallel to each other (Figure S4c).
To construct the $\scriptstyle1\mathrm{D}/1\mathrm{D}$ heterostructure, NU-1000 nanorods were used as seeds for the epitaxial growth of the PCN-222 nanorods. NU-1000 nanorods were synthesized based on previous reports54, 55 with some modifications (see the detailed Experimental Section in Supporting Information). The SEM and TEM images show the hexagonal prism-like morphology of NU-1000 nanorods with the length of $948\pm89\ \mathrm{nm}$ and width of $272\pm34~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S6a-d). The HRTEM image shows the obvious lattice fringes of NU-1000 nanorods (Figure S6e), and their XRD pattern matches well with the simulated pattern of NU-1000 MOF (Figure S6f), confirming their crystal structure. The hexagonal prism structure of the NU-1000 nanorods expose different facets (Inset in Figure S6a), i.e., (010) and (100) planes at the side and (001) plane in the end.56, 57 As shown in Figures 2a and S1b, PCN-222 nanorods were selectively grown on the (001) plane (end facets) of NU-1000 nanorods, rather than on other planes. This facet-selective growth is originated from the smaller lattice mismatch between the parameter a (or $b$ ) of the two MOFs than that of the lattice parameter $\boldsymbol{c}$ (Figure S7a, b).
Therefore, PCN-222 nanorods epitaxially grew along the $c$ axis of NU-1000, i.e., grown on two end facets of NU-1000 nanorods (Figure 2b). The TEM image of a typical heterostructure clearly shows the vertical growth of PCN-222 nanorods on the end facets of NU-1000 nanorods (Figure 2c). The HRTEM image of the interface shows the lattice fringes of NU-1000 and PCN-222 (Figure 2d), matching well with their crystal structures of (010) planes.49, 50 The continuous lattice fringes across the interface between NU-1000 and PCN-222 can be observed clearly, indicating that the epitaxial relationship between the NU-1000 and PCN-222 is $(010)_{\mathrm{NU}}.$ $_{1000}\|(010)_{\mathrm{PCN}-222}$ . The FFT analysis of NU-1000 and the interface displays quite similar patterns with the same orientation, further confirming the epitaxial growth of PCN222 nanorods along the $c$ axis of NU-1000 (Figure 2e). The crystal structure of PCN-222/NU-1000 heterostructure was further proved by XRD and SAXS results (Figures 2f and S8), which clearly show the diffraction peaks of both PCN-222 (marked with black stars) and NU-1000 (marked with red stars). Based on these results, the structure model can be drawn, in which PCN-222 crystals grow along the $c$ axis of
NU-1000 with their (010) planes parallel to each other (Figure S7c).
Furthermore, NU-1000 MOFs with different sizes and shapes were also used as seeds for the growth of PCN-222 nanorods (Figure S9). As shown in Figures S9a, c and e, the spindle-like NU-1000 MOFs with different lengths  (e.g. 482 $\pm26\mathrm{{nm}}$ , $1.36\pm0.11~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , and $2.51\pm0.32\upmu\mathrm{m})$ and widths (e.g. $233\pm21\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $0.61\pm0.04~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , and $1.52\:\pm\:0.13\:\:\upmu\mathrm{m})$ , were successfully synthesized. Importantly, the selective growth of PCN-222 nanorods on the ends of all NU-1000 MOFs were clearly observed (Figure S9b, d and f), indicating the universality of our seeded epitaxial growth method.
Different from the $1\mathrm{D}/0\mathrm{D}$ and $\scriptstyle1\mathrm{D}/1\mathrm{D}$ heterostructures, the construction of $\scriptstyle1\mathrm{D}/2\mathrm{D}$ heterostructures using 2D MOFs as seeds is synthetically challenging, as it requires the combination of different growth modes as well as the existence of discontinuous 1D/2D interfaces.10 In addition, the synthesis of 2D MOF seeds with controlled size and thickness still remains difficult.58 Here, we develop a method to prepare uniform hexagonal PCN-134 nanoplates (see the detailed
Experimental Section in Supporting Information), which have two basal planes and six edge planes. SEM, TEM and atomic force microscope (AFM) images show the 2D morphology of PCN-134 nanoplates with the edge length of $930\pm93~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and thickness of $65\pm13~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Figure S10a-e). The crystal structure of PCN-134 nanoplates is verified by HRTEM and XRD analysis (Figure $\operatorname{Sl0f-g)}$ . Owning to the difference in topological structures, PCN-134 and PCN-222 possess different crystal parameters. Interestingly, the parameter a of PCN-222 is almost two times that of PCN-134 (Figure S11a, b). Therefore, they have very small lattice mismatch
along the $\boldsymbol{c}$ axis according to the coincience site lattice mismatch model.59, 60 As expected, PCN-222 nanorods can selectively grow on the basal planes of PCN-134 nanoplates (Figures 3a-c and S1c). The scanning transmission electron microscope (STEM) image of a standing PCN-222/PCN-134 heterostructure clearly displays the vertical growth of the PCN-222 nanorods on both sides of PCN-134 nanoplates (inset in Figure 3b). The crystal structure of PCN-222/PCN134 heterostructure is confirmed by XRD and SAXS patterns (Figure S12), which clearly show the diffraction peaks of PCN-222 (marked with black stars) and PCN-134 (marked with red stars).
Since PCN-222 nanorods in the 1D/0D PCN-222/PCN-608 and 1D/1D PCN-222/NU-1000 heterostructures tend to lie on the TEM grids, it is very difficult to observe the lattice structure of (001) plane by TEM. However, PCN-222 nanorods vertically grew on the basal planes of PCN-134 nanoplates, making it possible to observe of the (001) plane.
The HRTEM image of the heterostructure clearly shows the hexagonal lattices of PCN-222 and PCN-134 (Figure 3d), which match well with their crystal structures of (001) planes,50, 52 indicating the facet-selective growth of PCN-222 nanorods on the (001) plane of PCN-134 nanoplates. In addition, the measured lattice fringes are 1.7 and $3.4\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which can be ascribed to the (010) planes of PCN-134 and PCN-222,50, 52 respectively. Based on the HRTEM measurement, it can be deduced that the epitaxial relationship between the PCN-134 and PCN-222 is $(010)_{\mathrm{PCN-}134}\|(010)_{\mathrm{PCN-}}$ 222 and $(001)_{\mathrm{PCN-1}34}\|(001)_{\mathrm{PCN-2}22}$ , which were further confirmed by FFT and selected-area electron diffraction (SAED). The FFT pattern of PCN-134 shows six spots, while the heterostructure shows six more spots with the same orientation, which can be attributed to PCN-222 nanorods, indicating the epitaxial growth of PCN-222 on PCN-134 (Figure 3e). Importantly, the SAED pattern taken at a piece of the heterostructure shows the diffraction spots clearly, which can be ascribed to the (010) and (020) planes of PCN-222 and the (010) plane of PCN-134 (Figures 3f and S13). The diffraction spots of PCN-222 nanorods have the same orientation as that of PCN-134, indicating that they selectively and epitaxially grow on the (001) plane of PCN-134 nanoplates. On the basis of the aforementioned observation and analysis, the epitaxial relationship between the PCN-134 and PCN-222 is confirmed to be $(010)_{\mathrm{PCN-134}}\Vert(010)_{\mathrm{PCN-222}}$ and $(001)_{\mathrm{PCN-134}}\|(001)_{\mathrm{PCN-222}}$ . Based on HRTEM, FFT and SAED results, the structure model can be drawn, in which PCN-222 crystals grow along the $c$ axis of PCN-134 with their (010) and (001) planes parallel to each other (Figure S11c).
As mentioned above, different oriented hierarchical MOF heterostructures have been successfully prepared by varying the structures, sizes, and morphologies of the MOF seeds. In addition to the seed engineering strategy, changing the structure of the secondary MOFs is another way to prepare oriented hierarchical MOF heterostructures. As presented in Figures 4a and S1c, by using PCN-134 nanoplate seeds, $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - BTB nanosheets epitaxially grew on their edge facets to form hierarchical 2D/2D heterostructures. Note that $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB is a layered MOF, and PCN-134 can be regarded as $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB MOF with intercalated TCPP ligands between the layers (Figure S14). Therefore, it is possible for the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB nanosheets to epitaxially grow on PCN-134 nanoplates. However, $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB has strong intra-layered coordination bonds, but weak interlayered interactions.53 As a result, $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB nanosheets grow faster on the edges of PCN-134 nanoplates through the coordination of BTB ligands with the metal nods than their growth on the basal planes of PCN-134, leading to the selective growth of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB on the edges of PCN-134 nanoplates, which have been confirmed by their SEM and STEM images (Figures 4b, c and S15). Importantly, the highangle annular dark field STEM (HAADF-STEM) image of the interface shows the continuity of lattice fringes, which can be ascribed to the (010) planes of PCN-134 and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB (Figure 4d),52, 53 indicating the epitaxial relationship between the PCN134 and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB is $(010)_{\mathrm{PCN-134}}\Vert(010)_{\mathrm{Zr-BTB}}.$ . The FFT analysis at the interface and PCN-134 of the heterostructure displays quite similar patterns with the same orientation, further confirming the epitaxial growth relationship between $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB and PCN-134 (Figure 4e). Due to the overlap of diffraction peaks, it is difficult to distinguish the peaks of PCN-134 and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB in the heterostructure. However, the intensity of the (010) plane in the heterostructure increased obviously, compared with that of pure PCN-134 nanoplates (Figures 4f and $\mathbf{S}10\mathbf{g}$ ), further confirming the successful synthesis of $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - BTB/PCN-134 heterostructure. Based on these results, the structure model can be drawn, in which $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB MOFs grow on the edge of PCN-134 with their (010) planes parallel to each other (Figure S14c).
Furthermore, the pore sizes of the obtained heterostructures were measured. As shown in Figure S16, the MOF heterostructures present the typical pores of MOF seeds and secondary MOFs, indicating that they have been successfully synthesized.
Importantly, our method is general, which can also be used to prepare oriented hierarchical MOF heterostructures based on other metals, such as aluminum (Al) and indium (In). As shown in Figure S17, Al-TCPP nanoplates were selectively grown on the edge of MIL-69 nanoribbons to form the oriented hierarchical heterostructures of Al-TCPP/MIL-69, and In-TCPP nanoplates were selectively grown on the side surfaces of MIL-68 rods to form In-TCPP/MIL-68 heterostructures.
## 3. CONCLUSION
In summary, we have proposed and successfully demonstrated the synthesis of a series of oriented hierarchical MOF heterostructures via the seeded epitaxial growth method. MOFs with rationally designed structures and lattice parameters are deliberately used to achieve the highly controlled growth of hierarchical MOF heterostructures with specific orientations, in which the secondary MOFs only epitaxially grows on the specific surface of the MOF seeds with smaller lattice mismatch. By engineering the MOF seeds, a series of hierarchical MOF heterostructures, including 1D/0D PCN-222/PCN-6080D, 1D/1D PCN-222/NU-1000 and 1D/2D PCN-222/PCN-134, were successfully prepared. In addition, by engineering the structure of the secondary MOF, another hierarchical MOF heterostructure, i.e. 2D/2D ZrBTB/PCN-134, was also successfully prepared by the selective growth of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTB on the side facets of PCN-134. Both strategies require the highly control over the structures, sizes, dimensionalities, morphologies and lattice parameters of the MOF crystals. We believe that our as-demonstrated concept and method may pave the way for the rational design and preparation of hierarchical MOF heterostructures with various functionalities including multiple active sites, accessible interfaces, controlled surface properties, tunable bandgaps and hierarchical pores, which might have promising potentials in applications in multistep tandem catalysis, photocatalysis, and selective absorption and separation. For example, MOF heterostructures for tandem catalysis could be prepared by introducing different active sites in the MOF seeds and secondary MOFs. Owing to the readily accessible interfaces, MOF heterostructures also exhibit great potentials in designing high-performance photocatalysts by hybridization of different MOFs with suitable bandgaps to facilitate the charge transfer process. In addition, hierarchical MOF heterostructures with super hydrophobicity could be synthesized by using hydrophobic ligands, which may enhance the adsorption and separation of organic compounds (e.g., hydrocarbon compounds, oil pollutants and organic reactants).
